<td id="A1.T7.3.4.10" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="A1.T7.3.4.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">72.4</span></td>
<td id="A1.T7.3.4.11" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="A1.T7.3.4.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">57.8</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 7: </span><span id="A1.T7.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effect of discriminative <em id="A1.T7.5.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">vs</em>.<span id="A1.T7.5.2" class="ltx_text"></span> generative training on the TAP-Vid (top-left), PointOdyssey (top-right, left), Dynamic Replica (top-right, right), and TAP-Vid sliding occluder (bottom) benchmarks</span>. Each model is trained in a shortened training setup. TAP-Vid results are measured in <math id="A1.T7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta^{\text{vis}}_{\text{avg}}\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mtext>avg</mtext><mtext>vis</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta^{\text{vis}}_{\text{avg}}\ </annotation></semantics></math>(<math id="A1.T7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math>).</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS10" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.10 </span>Effect of varying <math id="A1.SS10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\text{coord}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>coord</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\text{coord}}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="A1.SS10.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here we study the effect on tracking accuracy when varying the standard deviation <math id="A1.SS10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\text{coord}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>coord</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\text{coord}}</annotation></semantics></math> of the trajectory priors and conditional probability path. Table <a href="#A1.T8" title='Table 8 ‣ A.10 Effect of varying 𝜎_"coord" ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Generative Point Tracking with Flow Matching' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> shows our quantitative results. For each value of <math id="A1.SS10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\text{coord}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>coord</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\text{coord}}</annotation></semantics></math> we train a separate model in a shortened training setup on PointOdyssey, where we train for 100K steps and use a total batch size of two. In short summary, increasing <math id="A1.SS10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\text{coord}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>coord</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\text{coord}}</annotation></semantics></math> results in lower tracking accuracy. For our main model, we chose <math id="A1.SS10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\text{coord}}=0.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>coord</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\text{coord}}=0.25</annotation></semantics></math> as we found it allows for a healthy amount of mode exploration during training while minimizing noise that may throw the tracker off track when the solution space is unimodal (<em id="A1.SS10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="A1.SS10.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span> when the point is easy to track).</p>
</div>
